From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: liviyifan <344781483+liviyifan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 20:16:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: ilp32: SVE and SME for ILP32 tasks

ILP32 tasks run AArch64 code and get the SVE and SME hwcaps
(cpu_get_elf_hwcap()), but the SVE and SME code refused them as compat
tasks:

- do_sve_acc() and do_sme_acc() warned and sent SIGILL on the first SVE
  or SME instruction. A C library that picks SVE string functions by
  HWCAP_SVE (glibc 2.41) made every ILP32 program die with SIGILL on a
  CPU with SVE.
- PR_SVE_SET_VL, PR_SVE_GET_VL, PR_SME_SET_VL and PR_SME_GET_VL returned
  -EINVAL.
- tls_preserve_current_state() did not save TPIDR2_EL0, which
  tls_thread_switch() restores, so an ILP32 task lost TPIDR2 at a
  context switch.

Refuse only AArch32 tasks there: is_a32_compat_task(). The signal frame
code (signal_common.h) and the ptrace SVE, SSVE and ZA regsets are shared
with LP64 and already handle ILP32 tasks.

Tested on QEMU -cpu max (SVE, vector lengths up to 256 bytes): ILP32
programs built with GCC 14 and glibc 2.41 run (they died with SIGILL
before), and an ILP32 test passes as the same LP64 one: the SVE registers
are in the signal frame and back after sigreturn, at vector lengths 64
and 16; PR_SVE_GET_VL and PR_SVE_SET_VL; ptrace NT_ARM_SVE.
ARM64_SME depends on BROKEN in 6.12.y, so the SME parts are not built
here and were not tested.

Signed-off-by: liviyifan <344781483+liviyifan@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 arch/arm64/kernel/fpsimd.c  | 12 ++++++------
 arch/arm64/kernel/process.c |  2 +-
 2 files changed, 7 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/fpsimd.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/fpsimd.c
@@ -957,7 +957,7 @@ int sve_set_current_vl(unsigned long arg
 	vl = arg & PR_SVE_VL_LEN_MASK;
 	flags = arg & ~vl;
 
-	if (!system_supports_sve() || is_compat_task())
+	if (!system_supports_sve() || is_a32_compat_task())
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	ret = vec_set_vector_length(current, ARM64_VEC_SVE, vl, flags);
@@ -970,7 +970,7 @@ int sve_set_current_vl(unsigned long arg
 /* PR_SVE_GET_VL */
 int sve_get_current_vl(void)
 {
-	if (!system_supports_sve() || is_compat_task())
+	if (!system_supports_sve() || is_a32_compat_task())
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	return vec_prctl_status(ARM64_VEC_SVE, 0);
@@ -986,7 +986,7 @@ int sme_set_current_vl(unsigned long arg
 	vl = arg & PR_SME_VL_LEN_MASK;
 	flags = arg & ~vl;
 
-	if (!system_supports_sme() || is_compat_task())
+	if (!system_supports_sme() || is_a32_compat_task())
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	ret = vec_set_vector_length(current, ARM64_VEC_SME, vl, flags);
@@ -999,7 +999,7 @@ int sme_set_current_vl(unsigned long arg
 /* PR_SME_GET_VL */
 int sme_get_current_vl(void)
 {
-	if (!system_supports_sme() || is_compat_task())
+	if (!system_supports_sme() || is_a32_compat_task())
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	return vec_prctl_status(ARM64_VEC_SME, 0);
@@ -1388,7 +1388,7 @@ static void sve_init_regs(void)
 void do_sve_acc(unsigned long esr, struct pt_regs *regs)
 {
 	/* Even if we chose not to use SVE, the hardware could still trap: */
-	if (unlikely(!system_supports_sve()) || WARN_ON(is_compat_task())) {
+	if (unlikely(!system_supports_sve()) || WARN_ON(is_a32_compat_task())) {
 		force_signal_inject(SIGILL, ILL_ILLOPC, regs->pc, 0);
 		return;
 	}
@@ -1430,7 +1430,7 @@ void do_sve_acc(unsigned long esr, struc
 void do_sme_acc(unsigned long esr, struct pt_regs *regs)
 {
 	/* Even if we chose not to use SME, the hardware could still trap: */
-	if (unlikely(!system_supports_sme()) || WARN_ON(is_compat_task())) {
+	if (unlikely(!system_supports_sme()) || WARN_ON(is_a32_compat_task())) {
 		force_signal_inject(SIGILL, ILL_ILLOPC, regs->pc, 0);
 		return;
 	}
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/process.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/process.c
@@ -431,7 +431,7 @@ int copy_thread(struct task_struct *p, c
 void tls_preserve_current_state(void)
 {
 	*task_user_tls(current) = read_sysreg(tpidr_el0);
-	if (system_supports_tpidr2() && !is_compat_task())
+	if (system_supports_tpidr2() && !is_a32_compat_task())
 		current->thread.tpidr2_el0 = read_sysreg_s(SYS_TPIDR2_EL0);
 }
 
